{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01S8jyHhbiXLyyKZdQWSDuET","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016bCHubs8czMhWrenXEg647","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD\"\n\nThe paper studies why training-free LLM IE degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a **multi-channel degradation model**: Channel 1 (Count → Recall decline via capacity bottleneck) and Channel 2 (Confusability → Type confusion via semantic interference). These are claimed to be orthogonal and additive. The paper then proposes SDGD (Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition), a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n\n## Key Claims\n1. BCS independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial r=0.32, p=0.002 on Few-NERD; r=0.22, p=0.004 on MAVEN)\n2. Count and confusability operate through orthogonal channels (2×2 factorial, interaction p=0.82; error taxonomy crossover χ²=142.3, p=2.1e-24)\n3. SDGD improves F1 by +7.6 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass, matching per-type splitting at 22% latency cost\n\n## Datasets & Models\n- Few-NERD (66 NER types), MAVEN (168 event types), DocRED (96 relation types, supplementary)\n- Qwen3-32B (primary), LLaMA-3.3-70B (cross-family validation)\n\n## Key Tables (from the paper):\n\n**Table 1: MAVEN count curve (Qwen3-32B)**\nCount=1: F1=0.33, Prec=0.22, Recall=0.66, WT=0\nCount=5: F1=0.29, Prec=0.23, Recall=0.39, WT=420\nCount=15: F1=0.24, Prec=0.24, Recall=0.24, WT=1850\nCount=30: F1=0.19, Prec=0.24, Recall=0.16, WT=3600\nCount=60: F1=0.13, Prec=0.22, Recall=0.09, WT=6200\nCount=120: F1=0.08, Prec=0.18, Recall=0.05, WT=9100\nCount=168: F1=0.06, Prec=0.15, Recall=0.04, WT=11500\n\n**Table 2: Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation**\nFew-NERD: NER, 1953 pairs, r=0.156, p=4.6e-12, ρ_nz=0.254\nMAVEN: ED, 14028 pairs, r=0.19, p=8.4e-10, ρ_nz=0.23\n\n**Table 3: Per-type partial correlation r(F1, mean_BCS | log_freq)**\nFew-NERD: 66 types, partial r=0.32, p=0.002\nMAVEN: 168 types, partial r=0.22, p=0.004\n\n**Table 4: Multi-model comparison (Few-NERD)**\nQwen3-32B: r=0.156, p=4.6e-12, ρ_nz=0.254\nLLaMA-3.3-70B: r=0.14, p=3.2e-10, ρ_nz=0.22, Top-10 overlap=7/10\n\n**Table 5: 2×2 factorial (Few-NERD, Qwen3-32B)**\nLow Count/Low BCS: F1=0.42, Recall=0.48, TCR=0.5%\nLow Count/High BCS: F1=0.36, Recall=0.46, TCR=4.5%\nHigh Count/Low BCS: F1=0.34, Recall=0.28, TCR=1.2%\nHigh Count/High BCS: F1=0.28, Recall=0.26, TCR=5.8%\nCount main effect on F1: -0.08; BCS main effect: -0.06; Interaction ≈ 0 (F=0.05, p=0.82)\n\n**Table 6: Error taxonomy by BCS bin (Few-NERD, n=1191 errors)**\n[0,0.2): TypeSub=8%, BndErr=12%, FalseNeg=62%, Halluc=18%, n=245\n[0.2,0.4): TypeSub=15%, BndErr=10%, FalseNeg=55%, Halluc=20%, n=312\n[0.4,0.6): TypeSub=35%, BndErr=8%, FalseNeg=38%, Halluc=19%, n=280\n[0.6,0.8): TypeSub=55%, BndErr=6%, FalseNeg=24%, Halluc=15%, n=198\n[0.8,1.0]: TypeSub=68%, BndErr=5%, FalseNeg=15%, Halluc=12%, n=156\nχ²=142.3, p=2.1e-24\n\n**Table 7: Full comparison on Few-NERD (66 types)**\nSingle-pass: F1=0.414, Prec=0.648, Recall=0.304, WT=2434, API=1, ∥s=48\nRandom(k=10): F1=0.455, Prec=0.49, Recall=0.425, WT=1580, API=10, ∥s=90\nSDGD-zero(k=10): F1=0.490, Prec=0.52, Recall=0.462, WT=680, API=10, ∥s=95\nSDGD-cal(k=9): F1=0.505, Prec=0.53, Recall=0.483, WT=520, API=17, ∥s=130\nPer-type(k=66): F1=0.490, Prec=0.44, Recall=0.550, WT=0, API=66, ∥s=420\n\n**Table 8: Full comparison on MAVEN (168 types)**\nSingle-pass: F1=0.08, Prec=0.15, Recall=0.05, WT=11500, k=1, API=1\nRandom(k=18): F1=0.11, Prec=0.14, Recall=0.095, WT=5800, k=18, API=18\nSDGD-zero(k=18): F1=0.14, Prec=0.16, Recall=0.125, WT=3200, k=18, API=18\nSDGD-cal(k=16): F1=0.16, Prec=0.175, Recall=0.148, WT=2400, k=16, API=26\nPer-type(k=168): F1=0.145, Prec=0.13, Recall=0.165, WT=450, k=168, API=168\n\n**Table 9: Gain decomposition on Few-NERD**\nCount reduction (Random-SP): ΔF1=+4.1pp, ΔRecall=+12.1pp, ΔWT=-35%\nConfusability reduction (SDGD-Random): ΔF1=+3.5pp, ΔRecall=+3.7pp, ΔWT=-57%\nTotal (SDGD-SP): ΔF1=+7.6pp, ΔRecall=+15.8pp, ΔWT=-72%\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what the full experiment plan includes)\n\nThe paper's experiment plan includes these experiments:\n1. MAVEN Cross-Task Confusability Replication (exp-004) — count curve + pairwise BCS analysis + per-type partial correlation on MAVEN. **Covered in paper.**\n2. DocRED Cross-Task Scaling Stress Test (exp-005) — count curve + BCS correlation on DocRED (96 relation types). **Covered briefly in Section 5.5.**\n3. Count × Confusability 2×2 Interaction (exp-006) — factorial design on Few-NERD. **Covered in paper (Table 5).**\n4. BCS-Binned Error Taxonomy (exp-007) — error type distribution across BCS bins. **Covered in paper (Table 6).**\n5. Multi-Model Validation with LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012) — Few-NERD replication. **Covered in paper (Table 4).**\n6. SDGD-zero on Few-NERD (exp-008) — graph coloring decomposition. **Covered (Table 7).**\n7. SDGD-zero on MAVEN (exp-009) — cross-task SDGD validation. **Covered (Table 8).**\n8. SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (exp-010) — calibration-based variant. **Covered (Table 7).**\n9. SDGD-cal on MAVEN (exp-011) — calibration on MAVEN. **Covered (Table 8).**\n10. Random Batching Ablation (exp-013) — gain decomposition. **Covered (Tables 7, 9).**\n11. Per-Type Splitting Ablation (exp-014) — upper bound comparison. **Covered (Tables 7, 8).**\n12. SCS Embedding Proxy Validation (exp-015) — SCS vs empirical confusion. **Covered in Section 5.6.**\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental rigor)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental gaps)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, answerable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** — score (1-6) + 2-sentence justification\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Is the experimental design sound? Are confounds properly controlled?\n- Are effect sizes meaningful or just statistically significant?\n- Is the evaluation comprehensive enough?\n- Are there missing baselines or ablations?\n- How robust are the results? Single run or multiple seeds?\n- Are the absolute performance numbers concerning?\n- Is the statistical methodology appropriate?\n- Coverage of the imagined experiment list (all 12 experiments appear covered)\n\nOutput your full review as structured text."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39241,"cache_read_input_tokens":22927,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39241},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fedf8bd9-cdbd-4afd-b68e-e46304bb8fb5","uuid":"f4f838f1-cbfb-45ea-8529-952c33138786"}
