{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TJnqpJ2q58SMRHcUQ5ujqy","type":"tool_result","content":"300\tType substitution errors increase monotonically from 18\\% (low BCS) to 52\\% (high BCS), while false negatives decrease from 55\\% to 28\\%. This directly confirms the multi-channel model: confusability drives type confusion (Channel~2) while count/attention dilution drives entity omission (Channel~1). The association between type substitution rate and BCS bin is statistically significant ($\\chi^2=18.4$, $p=0.001$).\n301\t\n302\tBoundary errors remain approximately flat across BCS bins (11--15\\%), confirming they are driven by surface-form ambiguity rather than type confusability. Hallucination rates show a slight decrease (12\\% to 7\\%) that is not monotonic and not significant ($p=0.18$). We characterize these as the \\emph{dominant error modes shifting} with confusability, rather than exclusive channels.\n303\t\n304\t%==============================================================================\n305\t\\section{Claim 3: SDGD Effectiveness}\n306\t\\label{sec:claim3}\n307\t%==============================================================================\n308\t\n309\t\\subsection{SDGD Results}\n310\t\n311\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_main} reports the full comparison across methods and datasets.\n312\t\n313\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n314\t\\centering\n315\t\\small\n316\t\\caption{Full comparison across methods. SDGD-cal achieves 95--99\\% of per-type quality at $\\sim$20\\% of the API cost. $p$ values are vs.\\ the best method (SDGD-cal).}\n317\t\\label{tab:sdgd_main}\n318\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccr}\n319\t\\toprule\n320\tDataset & Method & $k$ & F1 & Prec. & Recall & WrongType & API Calls \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t\\multirow{5}{*}{Few-NERD (66)} & Single-pass & 1 & .414 & .648 & .304 & 2,050 & 500 \\\\\n323\t & Random ($k$=10) & 10 & .423 & .395 & .455 & 1,650 & 5,000 \\\\\n324\t & SDGD-zero & 10 & .437 & .405 & .474 & 1,280 & 5,000 \\\\\n325\t & SDGD-cal & 11 & \\textbf{.449} & .415 & .488 & 1,100 & 5,528 \\\\\n326\t & Per-type & 66 & .470 & .385 & .602 & 650 & 33,000 \\\\\n327\t\\midrule\n328\t\\multirow{5}{*}{MAVEN (168)} & Single-pass & 1 & .135 & .340 & .082 & 11,500 & 710 \\\\\n329\t & Random ($k$=22) & 22 & .155 & .175 & .138 & 6,800 & 15,620 \\\\\n330\t & SDGD-zero & 22 & .175 & .190 & .162 & 4,200 & 15,620 \\\\\n331\t & SDGD-cal & 24 & \\textbf{.192} & .205 & .180 & 3,500 & 16,964 \\\\\n332\t & Per-type & 168 & .195 & .150 & .280 & 1,800 & 119,280 \\\\\n333\t\\bottomrule\n334\t\\end{tabular}\n335\t\\end{table*}\n336\t\n337\tOn MAVEN, SDGD-zero yields $+4.0$ F1 over single-pass ($p=0.002$) and $+2.0$ over random batching ($p=0.038$). SDGD-cal further improves to F1$=0.192$, nearly matching per-type splitting ($0.195$, $p=0.82$) at 14\\% of the API cost (16,964 vs.\\ 119,280 calls).\n338\t\n339\tOn Few-NERD, gains are more modest: SDGD-zero yields $+2.3$ F1 over single-pass ($p=0.003$), consistent with the smaller schema (66 types) providing less room for confusability-aware optimization.\n340\t\n341\t\\subsection{Scaling with Schema Complexity}\n342\t\n343\tThe confusability-aware contribution scales with schema size. Table~\\ref{tab:cross_dataset_gain} summarizes gains across datasets.\n344\t\n345\t\\begin{table}[t]\n346\t\\centering\n347\t\\small\n348\t\\caption{SDGD gains scale with schema size: larger schemas benefit more from confusability-aware decomposition.}\n349\t\\label{tab:cross_dataset_gain}\n350\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n351\t\\toprule\n352\tDataset & Types & $\\Delta$F1 & $\\Delta$WrongType \\\\\n353\t\\midrule\n354\tFew-NERD & 66 & $+$2.3 & $-$37.5\\% \\\\\n355\tMAVEN & 168 & $+$4.0 & $-$63.5\\% \\\\\n356\t\\bottomrule\n357\t\\end{tabular}\n358\t\\end{table}\n359\t\n360\tWrong-type errors decrease by 37.5\\% on Few-NERD and 63.5\\% on MAVEN, confirming that confusability-aware grouping directly targets the type confusion mechanism.\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Random Batching Ablation}\n363\t\n364\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_random} isolates the two contributions of SDGD: batch size reduction (Channel~1 mitigation, captured by random batching) and confusability-aware grouping (Channel~2 mitigation, the SDGD-specific contribution).\n365\t\n366\t\\begin{table}[t]\n367\t\\centering\n368\t\\small\n369\t\\caption{Random vs.\\ SDGD ablation. On MAVEN, confusability-aware grouping contributes $+2.0$ F1 beyond mere batch count reduction ($p=0.018$).}\n370\t\\label{tab:ablation_random}\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\tDataset & Single & Random & SDGD & $p$ \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\tFew-NERD & .414 & .423$\\pm$.008 & .437 & .08 \\\\\n376\tMAVEN & .135 & .155$\\pm$.006 & .175 & .018 \\\\\n377\t\\bottomrule\n378\t\\end{tabular}\n379\t\\end{table}\n380\t\n381\tOn MAVEN, the SDGD-specific contribution ($+2.0$ over random) is significant ($p=0.018$) and equals the batch-reduction contribution ($+2.0$ from single to random). SDGD reduces mean within-batch BCS by 38--41\\% compared to random batching (0.145 vs.\\ 0.235 on MAVEN).\n382\t\n383\tOn Few-NERD, the SDGD-specific contribution ($+1.4$) is marginal ($p=0.08$), consistent with fewer high-SCS pairs in a 66-type schema.\n384\t\n385\t\\subsection{Per-Type Splitting as Upper Bound}\n386\t\n387\tPer-type splitting (one type per batch) provides a quality upper bound but is cost-prohibitive. On MAVEN, SDGD-cal achieves 98.5\\% of per-type F1 (0.192 vs.\\ 0.195) at 14\\% of the API cost (Table~\\ref{tab:sdgd_main}). Per-type splitting also suffers from severe merge conflicts: 72\\% of documents on Few-NERD and 85\\% on MAVEN have span conflicts across passes, introducing resolution errors.\n388\t\n389\t\\subsection{SDGD-cal: Calibration-Enhanced Decomposition}\n390\t\n391\tSDGD-cal replaces embedding SCS with an empirical confusion matrix estimated from 8 calibration examples. On MAVEN, this yields $+1.7$ F1 over SDGD-zero ($p=0.045$), discovering 128 empirical confusion pairs with only 48\\% overlap with SCS top-100 predictions (Table~\\ref{tab:sdgd_cal}).\n392\t\n393\t\\begin{table}[t]\n394\t\\centering\n395\t\\small\n396\t\\caption{SDGD-cal vs.\\ SDGD-zero. Calibration provides larger gains on MAVEN where SCS is less accurate ($\\rho=0.34$ vs.\\ $0.42$ on Few-NERD).}\n397\t\\label{tab:sdgd_cal}\n398\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n399\t\\toprule\n400\tMethod & F1 & Prec. & Recall & Cal.\\ Cost \\\\\n401\t\\midrule\n402\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Few-NERD}} \\\\\n403\tSDGD-zero & .437 & .405 & .474 & 0 \\\\\n404\tSDGD-cal & .449 & .415 & .488 & 528 calls \\\\\n405\t$\\Delta$ & $+$1.2 & $+$1.0 & $+$1.4 & \\\\\n406\t\\midrule\n407\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{MAVEN}} \\\\\n408\tSDGD-zero & .175 & .190 & .162 & 0 \\\\\n409\tSDGD-cal & .192 & .205 & .180 & 1,344 calls \\\\\n410\t$\\Delta$ & $+$1.7 & $+$1.5 & $+$1.8 & \\\\\n411\t\\bottomrule\n412\t\\end{tabular}\n413\t\\end{table}\n414\t\n415\tThe gain is larger on MAVEN because SCS is a weaker proxy for FrameNet event types ($\\rho=0.34$) than for NER types ($\\rho=0.42$): frame-semantic distinctions (e.g., \\emph{Giving} vs.\\ \\emph{Commerce\\_sell}) are poorly captured by name embeddings.\n416\t\n417\t%==============================================================================\n418\t\\section{SCS Validation}\n419\t\\label{sec:scs_validation}\n420\t%==============================================================================\n421\t\n422\tWe validate the SCS proxy by comparing embedding-based similarities against empirical confusion matrices from calibration runs (Table~\\ref{tab:scs_valid}).\n423\t\n424\t\\begin{table}[t]\n425\t\\centering\n426\t\\small\n427\t\\caption{SCS validation: embedding similarity vs.\\ empirical confusion. SCS is a moderate proxy ($\\rho=0.38$), stronger on Few-NERD than MAVEN.}\n428\t\\label{tab:scs_valid}\n429\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n430\t\\toprule\n431\tMetric & Few-NERD & MAVEN & Overall \\\\\n432\t\\midrule\n433\tSpearman $\\rho$ & 0.42 & 0.34 & 0.38 \\\\\n434\tKendall $\\tau$ & 0.30 & 0.24 & 0.27 \\\\\n435\tTop-50 Precision & 0.68 & 0.56 & 0.62 \\\\\n436\tTop-50 Recall & 0.50 & 0.40 & 0.45 \\\\\n437\t\\bottomrule\n438\t\\end{tabular}\n439\t\\end{table}\n440\t\n441\tSCS overestimates confusability for type pairs with distinctive lexical cues (e.g., \\emph{org-religion} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{org-political}, SCS$=0.72$ but 0 empirical confusion---entity mentions like ``Church of X'' vs.\\ ``X Party'' are easily distinguished). SCS underestimates confusion for pairs sharing entity surface forms (e.g., \\emph{art-film} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{art-broadcastprogram}, SCS$=0.38$ but 12 confusions---titles like ``Breaking Bad'' can be either).\n442\t\n443\tThese failure modes explain why SDGD-cal improves over SDGD-zero: calibration corrects both false positives (35 overestimates) and false negatives (28 underestimates) in the SCS ranking.\n444\t\n445\t%==============================================================================\n446\t\\section{DocRED: A Boundary Condition}\n447\t\\label{sec:docred}\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t\n450\tWe test on DocRED (96 relation types) as a scaling stress test. The pairwise correlation is marginal ($r=0.055$, $p=0.065$). Recall degrades monotonically with count (0.21 at count$=1$ to 0.028 at count$=96$), confirming Channel~1 is task-agnostic. However, the confusability signal is obscured by task-specific confounds: document-level RE requires multi-hop reasoning and entity-pair identification, introducing noise that overwhelms the type confusion signal at the very low absolute F1 levels (0.04--0.10) observed in training-free RE. We interpret this as a boundary condition: confusability analysis requires sufficient base extraction quality for the signal to emerge.\n451\t\n452\t%==============================================================================\n453\t\\section{Discussion}\n454\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n455\t%==============================================================================\n456\t\n457\t\\paragraph{Effect size variation.}\n458\tThe pairwise BCS--confusion correlation varies from $r=0.156$ (Few-NERD, Qwen3-32B) to $r=0.09$ (MAVEN; LLaMA-3.3-70B). This reflects two factors: (1) larger schemas dilute per-pair confusion rates, reducing correlation magnitude, and (2) different models' internal representations align differently with static embedding SCS. The consistent \\emph{direction} across all conditions---positive correlation, same top confused pairs---is more informative than the varying magnitude.\n459\t\n460\t\\paragraph{Additivity with marginal interaction.}\n461\tThe non-significant interaction ($p=0.135$) supports the additive model, but the slight super-additive residual ($-0.005$) and $\\eta^2=0.04$ suggest that at higher counts, confusability's impact may be amplified. Our cell size ($n=12$) provides limited power to detect small interactions; larger-scale factorial designs could resolve this.\n462\t\n463\t\\paragraph{Applicability scope.}\n464\tSDGD's gains scale with schema complexity: significant on 168 types (MAVEN), marginal on 66 types (Few-NERD), and likely unnecessary below 30 types where single-pass performs adequately. The framework is most valuable when the schema exceeds the model's effective discrimination capacity.\n465\t\n466\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n467\t(1) Absolute F1 remains low---SDGD narrows the gap to supervised methods but does not close it; remaining errors stem from non-confusability sources.\n468\t(2) The API cost of multi-pass extraction (10--22$\\times$ single-pass) may be prohibitive for some applications, though SDGD-zero requires no calibration data.\n469\t(3) SCS as a confusability proxy has moderate accuracy ($\\rho=0.38$); better confusability measures (e.g., LLM-internal representations) could improve SDGD.\n470\t\n471\t%==============================================================================\n472\t\\section{Conclusion}\n473\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n474\t%==============================================================================\n475\t\n476\tWe present the first controlled study of multi-channel schema degradation in training-free LLM IE. Our findings show that type confusability contributes to extraction degradation beyond count, with strongest evidence at the type-pair mechanistic level. Count and confusability operate through largely additive channels---count drives recall decline, confusability drives type confusion---enabling independent mitigation. SDGD operationalizes confusability-aware decomposition via graph coloring, achieving significant gains on complex schemas ($+4.0$ F1 on MAVEN's 168 types) while matching per-type splitting quality at $\\sim$20\\% of the cost. The scaling pattern---larger schemas benefit more---suggests that SDGD's practical value will increase as IE applications target increasingly complex ontologies.\n477\t\n478\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n479\t\n480\t\n481\t\\end{document}\n482\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016bCHubs8czMhWrenXEg647","session_id":"fedf8bd9-cdbd-4afd-b68e-e46304bb8fb5","uuid":"e935e4de-b777-4da3-bfc9-348d74c86636","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:12:44.769Z"}
