{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RPq97oX7bbRguFZLboh3Xp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t% ============================================================\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms driving this degradation remain poorly understood.\n23\tWe conduct the first controlled study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} in schema-conditioned LLM IE.\n24\tAcross two tasks (NER on Few-NERD with 66 types; Event Detection on MAVEN with 168 types) and two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), we establish a \\textbf{multi-channel degradation model}: count drives recall decline through capacity bottleneck (Channel~1), while confusability drives type confusion errors through semantic interference (Channel~2).\n25\tBatch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with high-confusability batches exhibiting 10--28$\\times$ more type substitution errors.\n26\tWe operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}, a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n27\tSDGD improves F1 by 7.6 points on Few-NERD and 6.0 points on MAVEN over single-pass extraction, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t\n34\tLarge language models have become practical tools for training-free information extraction, enabling deployment in domains where annotated data is scarce or nonexistent---clinical records~\\cite{}, regulatory filings, and multilingual settings covering 50+ languages with no IE corpora.\n35\tHowever, performance degrades sharply as schema complexity increases: a 20--60 F1 gap separates training-free LLM IE from supervised systems on schemas with dozens of fine-grained types.\n36\t\n37\tExisting decomposition strategies address this gap with ad-hoc heuristics.\n38\tPer-type splitting~\\cite{} processes each type independently, eliminating inter-type confusion but losing cross-type context and incurring linear API cost.\n39\tSimilarity-based partitioning~\\cite{} uses type embeddings as implicit confusability proxies without empirical validation.\n40\tFixed pipeline decomposition~\\cite{} operates at the task level, ignoring intra-layer schema structure.\n41\tNone of these approaches isolates \\emph{what} makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n42\t\n43\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation is a \\emph{multi-channel} process.\n44\t\\textbf{Channel~1} (Count $\\to$ Recall): more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entity omission.\n45\t\\textbf{Channel~2} (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion): semantically similar co-present types interfere during type assignment, causing correct spans to receive wrong labels.\n46\tThese channels are orthogonal: reducing batch size addresses Channel~1; separating confusable types addresses Channel~2; the gains are additive.\n47\t\n48\tWe validate this model through three layers of evidence:\n49\t\\begin{enumerate}\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation} (immune to type frequency confounds) confirms that embedding-based confusability predicts empirical type confusion ($r{=}0.16$--$0.19$, $p{<}10^{-9}$) across 2 datasets and 2 models."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016bCHubs8czMhWrenXEg647","session_id":"fedf8bd9-cdbd-4afd-b68e-e46304bb8fb5","uuid":"370fb9db-2738-4b05-a076-c26530d17029","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:12:51.418Z"}
