{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UzTToiYWcs1nZ316G6h21P","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my complete review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 — Focus: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, proposing a two-channel degradation model that decomposes the problem into a count channel (driving recall decline via capacity bottleneck) and a confusability channel (driving type confusion via semantic interference between co-presented types). The authors validate this decomposition through controlled experiments on Few-NERD and MAVEN, then operationalize the finding into SDGD, a graph-coloring-based schema decomposition framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches. SDGD-zero achieves +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass extraction.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined research question with clean experimental isolation.** The controlled factorial design that holds count fixed while varying confusability (and vice versa) is methodologically rigorous and rare in the LLM IE literature. The \"Lost in the Middle\" analogy is apt -- this paper does for schema complexity what Liu et al. did for context length. The 2x2 factorial with interaction p=0.82 and the error taxonomy crossover (chi-squared=142.3) provide compelling evidence for the orthogonality claim.\n\n- **Genuinely novel decomposition of the \"schema complexity\" problem.** Prior work (LLM-PEE, UniNER, PARSE) has treated schema complexity as a monolithic challenge. The explicit isolation of count from confusability as independent degradation channels, supported by distinct error signatures (omission vs. type substitution), is a real conceptual contribution. The shift from \"schemas are too big\" to \"schemas contain confusable neighbors\" is a meaningful reframing.\n\n- **Transparent handling of confounds strengthens credibility.** The discovery that batch-level BCS-F1 correlation is *positive* (opposite to the hypothesis) due to type frequency confounding, and the subsequent pivot to pairwise and per-type analysis, is commendably reported. This kind of methodological honesty is rare and actually strengthens the paper's scientific contribution. The three-layer evidence hierarchy (pairwise > per-type > batch-level) is well-structured.\n\n- **SDGD is a natural and elegant operationalization.** Graph coloring on a thresholded confusability graph is a clean algorithmic translation of the empirical finding. The adaptive threshold (mean + k_sigma * std) avoids dataset-specific tuning. The two variants (zero-shot embedding-based vs. calibrated with 8-10 examples) offer practical flexibility.\n\n- **Cross-task validation.** Testing on both NER (Few-NERD, 66 types) and event detection (MAVEN, 168 types) demonstrates the finding is not task-specific. The 7/10 overlap in top confused pairs across model families (Qwen3 vs. LLaMA-3.3-70B) strengthens the claim that confusability is a schema property, not a model artifact.\n\n- **Good related work coverage.** The literature map is thorough, covering 25+ papers across schema decomposition, multi-agent IE, zero-shot NER/RE, and compact models. The positioning against CrossAgentIE (proactive vs. reactive) and LLM-PEE (confusability-optimized vs. similarity-partitioned) is clear and well-argued.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"multi-channel\" framing overstates the conceptual novelty.** That increasing the number of classes hurts recall and that semantically similar classes cause confusion are individually well-known phenomena in classification -- the former is the standard class-scaling problem, the latter is the foundation of confusion-matrix-based error analysis in supervised IE. The contribution is in demonstrating these hold for *training-free LLM IE* with *in-prompt schema presentation*, but the paper's framing as a \"discovery\" of a \"multi-channel degradation model\" implies more theoretical novelty than exists. The channels are not derived from a formal model of LLM behavior; they are post-hoc labels for two known failure modes observed in a controlled experiment. The paper should be more measured in distinguishing \"first controlled demonstration in this setting\" from \"discovery of a new phenomenon.\"\n\n- **Insufficient differentiation from LLM-PEE.** LLM-PEE already performs embedding-similarity-based type partitioning for LLM IE, which is mechanistically very close to SDGD-zero. The paper argues LLM-PEE uses similarity \"implicitly\" without validation, but the practical difference between \"partition by similarity to reduce prompt length\" and \"partition by confusability to reduce type confusion\" may be small -- both result in semantically dissimilar types being grouped together. The paper lacks a direct head-to-head comparison with LLM-PEE's partitioning algorithm. Without this, the claimed improvement over LLM-PEE remains a positioning argument rather than an empirical one.\n\n- **The \"first controlled study\" claim needs qualification.** IEPile (Chen et al., 2024) explicitly constructs hard-negative schemas (semantically proximate type sets) and studies their effect on extraction performance -- during training, but the experimental logic is analogous. The Familiarity metric (2025) also studies label similarity effects. The claim should be narrowed to \"first controlled study of confusability effects in *training-free, inference-time* LLM IE,\" which is accurate but less headline-worthy.\n\n- **Effect sizes are small and practically concerning.** The pairwise BCS-confusion correlation of r=0.156 (Few-NERD) and r=0.19 (MAVEN) explains ~2-4% of variance. The partial correlation on Few-NERD (r=0.32, p=0.002) is modest. The per-type partial correlation controlling for frequency (r=-0.200, p=0.116) is not significant at conventional thresholds. The SCS proxy validation (Spearman rho=0.52 and 0.48) shows the embedding proxy captures only about half the variance in empirical confusability. These are not damning, but the paper should be more candid about the predictive power of BCS as a standalone metric.\n\n- **Absolute performance levels undermine practical impact claims.** MAVEN F1 of 0.14-0.16 (even with SDGD) is far below any practical utility threshold. Few-NERD F1 of 0.49-0.51 is also below supervised baselines by 30+ points. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but continues to frame SDGD as a practical deployment tool. The practical narrative would be stronger if the paper positioned itself primarily as a *diagnostic and analytical* contribution, with SDGD as proof-of-concept operationalization rather than a production-ready method.\n\n- **Missing comparison with simple baselines.** The paper compares SDGD against single-pass and per-type splitting, but does not compare against (a) random batching with the same k (mentioned in the plan but results not reported in the paper summary), (b) LLM-PEE's actual partitioning, or (c) simple heuristics like alphabetical or hierarchical grouping. Without these, it is hard to assess how much of the gain comes from confusability-awareness specifically vs. any reasonable multi-batch strategy.\n\n- **Only 2 LLMs tested, both open-source 30-70B.** No proprietary API models (GPT-4, Claude) and no smaller models (<7B). The generalizability claim across model families rests on just Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B, which share similar architectural foundations (dense transformer, similar training paradigms). Testing on a fundamentally different model class (e.g., a proprietary API or a mixture-of-experts model) would significantly strengthen the claim.\n\n- **Graph coloring for schema decomposition is not novel in isolation.** Graph-based clustering and coloring for label partitioning appear in multi-label classification and structured prediction literature. The contribution is the specific application to LLM IE with confusability-weighted edges, but this should be acknowledged rather than presented as if graph coloring for type partitioning is a new idea.\n\n- **The paper uses [?] for all citations.** This is a serious formatting issue that would result in desk rejection at most venues. Every reference is broken. While this may be a compilation artifact, it prevents verification of citation accuracy and completeness.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **LLM-PEE comparison**: Can you provide a direct head-to-head comparison between SDGD-zero and LLM-PEE's similarity-based partitioning on the same datasets? What is the concrete algorithmic difference in partition quality?\n\n2. **Random batching ablation**: What is SDGD's gain over random batching with the same number of batches k? This is critical for isolating the confusability-aware signal from the general benefit of multi-batch extraction.\n\n3. **Batch-level positive correlation**: You report that batch-level BCS-F1 correlation is positive (opposite to hypothesis) due to frequency confounding. Does this mean that in practice, naively applying SDGD could *hurt* performance on high-frequency types by separating them from their naturally co-occurring (and high-BCS) neighbors?\n\n4. **Per-type partial correlation p=0.116**: The per-type analysis controlling for frequency fails to reach significance (p=0.116, n=63). How do you reconcile this with the strong claim that confusability independently predicts degradation? Is this an underpowered analysis or a genuine signal weakness?\n\n5. **DocRED partial correlation p=0.04**: You note this exceeds the 0.01 threshold. Does the multi-channel model replicate on relation extraction, or is confusability-driven degradation primarily an NER/ED phenomenon?\n\n6. **SCS proxy quality**: With Spearman rho of 0.48-0.52 between SCS and empirical confusion, roughly half the confusability signal is missed by embeddings. Have you analyzed what types of confusability SCS misses? Are there systematic failure modes (e.g., types that are semantically similar but contextually distinguishable)?\n\n7. **Interaction with prompt format**: Do your findings hold across different prompt formats (e.g., code-style as in GoLLIE vs. natural language descriptions)? Schema presentation format could modulate both count and confusability effects.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The title is very long and contains two colons. Consider shortening.\n- \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation\" could be misread as degradation of the schema itself rather than degradation caused by schema properties. Consider \"Schema-Induced Multi-Channel Degradation.\"\n- The BCS definition in Section 3.1 uses max pairwise SCS, but the experimental analysis appears to use average pairwise SCS. This inconsistency should be resolved.\n- The paper should clarify whether \"training-free\" means zero-shot (no task-specific examples) or few-shot (SDGD-cal uses 8-10 calibration examples). SDGD-cal is arguably few-shot.\n- The \"DAG\" in the title and SDGD acronym is misleading -- the confusability graph is undirected, not a DAG. Graph coloring on an undirected graph is the actual algorithm.\n- Error taxonomy percentages (TypeSub 8%->68%, FalseNeg 62%->15%) -- are these on the same total error count or normalized differently? The shift could partially reflect count differences.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThe paper asks a well-motivated question -- what specifically makes complex schemas hard for training-free LLM IE -- and provides a methodologically careful answer through controlled experiments that isolate count from confusability. The transparent handling of confounds (batch-level positive correlation) and the three-layer evidence structure demonstrate scientific rigor above the typical submission. The SDGD framework is a clean operationalization of the findings. However, the novelty is more incremental than the framing suggests: the individual phenomena (count-scaling degradation, similar-label confusion) are well-known, the core algorithmic contribution (embedding-similarity-based graph coloring for type partitioning) is close to LLM-PEE, and the effect sizes and absolute performance levels limit practical impact. The missing LLM-PEE comparison and the non-significant per-type partial correlation are notable gaps. This is a solid empirical contribution suitable for a venue like EMNLP Findings, but the novelty and impact may fall short of the main conference bar without strengthening the LLM-PEE differentiation and expanding the model coverage.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I am familiar with the training-free LLM IE literature, schema decomposition methods, and the specific competing works cited. I have read the related work section and method framework carefully. My main uncertainty is whether the full experimental results (which I reviewed from summaries rather than complete tables) contain additional evidence that addresses some of my concerns."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4d28aaedc0259637 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4d28aaedc0259637' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 47749\ntool_uses: 15\nduration_ms: 125785</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fedf8bd9-cdbd-4afd-b68e-e46304bb8fb5","uuid":"533822fe-bf2b-4359-88c0-527127ea9dd2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:14:42.806Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD\"\n\nThe paper studies why training-free LLM IE degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a **multi-channel degradation model**: Channel 1 (Count → Recall decline via capacity bottleneck) and Channel 2 (Confusability → Type confusion via semantic interference). These are claimed to be orthogonal and additive. The paper then proposes SDGD (Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition), a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n\n## Key Claims\n1. BCS independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial r=0.32, p=0.002 on Few-NERD; r=0.22, p=0.004 on MAVEN)\n2. Count and confusability operate through orthogonal channels (2×2 factorial, interaction p=0.82; error taxonomy crossover χ²=142.3, p=2.1e-24)\n3. SDGD improves F1 by +7.6 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass, matching per-type splitting at 22% latency cost\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\n**Training-Free LLM IE**: GoLLIE (code-style schemas), UniNER (per-type prompting), LLM-PEE (embedding similarity partitioning), ProUIE and KGGen (multi-step pipelines decomposing by task).\n\n**Schema Complexity Effects**: Negative impact of label-set size noted in passing but no controlled experiments isolating count from confusability. \"Lost in the Middle\" cited as methodological precedent for controlled LLM failure mode studies.\n\n**Type Confusability in IE**: Studied in supervised IE via confusion matrices. In training-free settings, confusability used implicitly (LLM-PEE) but never validated empirically as independent predictor.\n\n## Method\n- SCS (Schema Confusability Score): cosine similarity between type label embeddings\n- BCS (Batch Confusability Score): average pairwise SCS within a batch\n- Multi-channel model: ΔF1 ≈ α·Δ|B| + β·ΔBCS (additive, no interaction)\n- SDGD: (1) build confusability graph with SCS > τ edges, (2) greedy graph coloring → k batches, (3) independent extraction + dedup\n- SDGD-zero: embedding-only; SDGD-cal: uses 8-10 calibration examples for empirical confusion matrix\n\n## Key Results\n- Few-NERD: SDGD-zero F1=0.490 (+7.6 over single-pass 0.414), SDGD-cal F1=0.505 (+9.1)\n- MAVEN: SDGD-zero F1=0.14 (+6.0 over 0.08), SDGD-cal F1=0.16 (+8.0)\n- Pairwise BCS-confusion: r=0.156 (Few-NERD), r=0.19 (MAVEN), both p<1e-9\n- 2×2 factorial: no interaction (p=0.82), additive channels\n- Error taxonomy: TypeSub 8%→68% with BCS, FalseNeg 62%→15%, χ²=142.3\n- Multi-model: LLaMA-3.3-70B replicates pattern, 7/10 top confused pairs overlap\n- SCS proxy validation: Spearman ρ=0.52 (Few-NERD), 0.48 (MAVEN)\n\n## Datasets & Models\n- Few-NERD (66 NER types), MAVEN (168 event types), DocRED (96 relation types, supplementary)\n- Qwen3-32B (primary), LLaMA-3.3-70B (cross-family validation)\n\n## Limitations (from paper)\n- Only 2 primary models; no small models (<7B) or proprietary APIs\n- Absolute MAVEN quality (F1=0.14-0.16) far below supervised\n- τ selected via grid search; adaptive selection could help\n- DocRED per-type partial correlation p=0.04 > 0.01 threshold\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, contribution significance, positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty claims, related work gaps, framing issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, answerable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** — score (1-6) + 2-sentence justification\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Is the contribution genuinely novel? What exactly is new vs. incremental?\n- Is the \"multi-channel\" framing truly a contribution, or is it a restatement of known phenomena?\n- How does this compare to LLM-PEE which already uses embedding similarity for partitioning?\n- Is the related work coverage adequate? Missing references?\n- Is the \"first controlled study\" claim justified?\n- Is the graph-coloring approach for schema decomposition novel?\n- Does the paper adequately differentiate from prompt optimization / schema decomposition literature?\n- Is the practical impact significant given the low absolute F1 values?\n- How does this fit in the broader context of LLM IE research trends (e.g., fine-tuning, RAG, tool use)?\n- Are all citations present? (The paper uses [?] for all references, which is a formatting issue)\n\nOutput your full review as structured text.","agentId":"a4d28aaedc0259637","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my complete review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 — Focus: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, proposing a two-channel degradation model that decomposes the problem into a count channel (driving recall decline via capacity bottleneck) and a confusability channel (driving type confusion via semantic interference between co-presented types). The authors validate this decomposition through controlled experiments on Few-NERD and MAVEN, then operationalize the finding into SDGD, a graph-coloring-based schema decomposition framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches. SDGD-zero achieves +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass extraction.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined research question with clean experimental isolation.** The controlled factorial design that holds count fixed while varying confusability (and vice versa) is methodologically rigorous and rare in the LLM IE literature. The \"Lost in the Middle\" analogy is apt -- this paper does for schema complexity what Liu et al. did for context length. The 2x2 factorial with interaction p=0.82 and the error taxonomy crossover (chi-squared=142.3) provide